A||||6||Coop88||Rear Suspension Torque Specs||||5||
Z||000000||Coop88||05-27-2001||07:57 PM||||Anyone know what the torque specs are for the rear suspension on the 88s? I couldn't find it in my Haynes manual or the service manual. Almost have this thing back together, man I can't wait to drive it again.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||05-27-2001||08:08 PM||||Which torques do you need Coop?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Coop88||05-27-2001||09:10 PM||||I need the specs for all the trailing arms on the rear of my formula. There's three on each side. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||05-27-2001||09:39 PM||||Tight. Very Tight.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Coop88||05-27-2001||09:59 PM||||I'm using air tools, so do I need to be worried about the bolts being too tight? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||PBJ||05-27-2001||10:20 PM||||trailing arm frt to cradle. 37 lbs ft.<BR>trailing arm rear to knuckle 44 lbs ft. + 90 degrees<P>lateral control arm to cradle 41 lbs ft.<BR>lateral control arm to knuckle 37 lbs ft. + 90 degrees.<P>fixed adjustable link to cradle 41lbs. ft.<BR>fixed adjustable link to knuckle is same bolt as lateral control arm.<P>Hope this helps.<P>oh, and when you torque any bushing be sure vehicle suspension is sitting at ride hight. In other words torque with vehicle sitting on ground with tire on so you don't overstress rubber bushings.<p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 05-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Coop88||05-28-2001||12:14 AM||||Thanks! I'm putting in poly bushings w/KYBs, should be driving it very soon....||||reg||10
